 p  equals three and these are the three  cups that the empty removes water from  so that's what happens at every step and  again the emptier's goal is to minimize  the backlog which is the height of the  fullest cup  one of the classic algorithms for this  problem is the greedy algorithm  what does the greedy algorithm do well  in each step it just removes water  from the p fullest cups so the greedy  algorithm  is an algorithm for the emptier and  after a long line of research what we  know about the greedy algorithm  is that it achieves a pretty good  backlog it achieves a backlog  of big o of log n and it actually does  that no matter what the value of p  is moreover this turns out to actually  be asymptotically optimal  so no deterministic emptying algorithm  can do better  there's also been a whole bunch of other  directions of work on  on cup games so there's been work on  randomized algorithms  there's been work on smooth analysis  what happens if you randomly perturb  the starting state of the cup game  there's been work on restricted fillers  for example what happens if you consider  a filler that always puts the same  amount of water  into a given cup from step to step  there's been work on competitive  analysis resource augmentation  there's been other directions too such  as semi-clairvoyance and  you know other assumptions about the  empty and the filler  and what all of these research  directions have shared in common  is one common assumption which is that  the number of processors p is fixed  and so that's the assumption that we're  going to be playing with in this talk  what happens if we let p vary  and this is motivated in part by the  fact that on real systems it's becoming  increasingly the common case  that that many applications are running  on the same system at once  and that means from the perspective of a  single application the amount of  resources that are available to it  will vary over time the number of  processors available to it the amount of  cash available to it  the amount of memory available to it and  so on  one line of research that's been very  active recently has been trying to  understand what happens when the amount  of cash available to an application  fluctuates over time this has led to the  study of what are known as  cash adaptive algorithms but what's much  less well understood is what happens  if the number of available processors  fluctuates over time  and that's the question we want to  address today for the p processor cup  game  to do that we introduced this notion of  what we call the variable processor cup  game  and this works exactly like the standard  p processor cup game  except now the variable p the parameter  p  varies over time it's a function of the  step number t  and so besides that it's exactly the  same the only difference is that now  p changes over time  now one thing i want to point out is  that it isn't really fundamentally  necessary that we  that the filler should have the same  value of p as the emptier on each step  the reason we assume that though is that  way it's kind of a fair fight it makes  it so that  the amount of water the filler puts in  in each step is the same as the amount  of water that the emptier  can remove and so by making it a fair  fight  we make it a problem that where the  emptier has  where the emptier should be able to have  a chance of doing well  so actually when we started working on  this problem we actually thought this  would be kind of  a straightforward exercise and the  reason for that is that for each  individual value of p in the p processor  cup game  the optimal the optimal backlog is log n  in other words the optimal backlog has  nothing to do with your choice of p  and so we thought that for sure what  would happen is that  if you let p change over time the  optimal backlog would again  remain order log n  but what we were surprised to find was  our intuition was actually completely  wrong  so we ended up the first thing we ended  up showing is that the optimal backlog  bound that you can get for the greedy  emptying algorithm ends up being now  big o of n instead of big o of log n  so before the greedy emptying algorithm  achieved backlog  log n now it achieves backlog roughly  n so the backlog has exploded  but it turns out that's actually not the  greedy emptying algorithm's fault  and that's because it turns out now the  optimal backlog in the variable  processor cup game  actually is omega of n so for any  deterministic emptying algorithm  there's a corresponding filling strategy  that achieves backlog omega of n  within two to the n steps that's what we  prove  and so what that means is that actually  the greedy emptying algorithm is still  optimal  it's just that the optimal backlog now  is actually much larger than it was  before  so when we first proved this result we  were kind of shocked because we hadn't  expected these two games to be so  different than each other  and the first thing we wondered is we  said well this second theorem  this construction takes an awfully long  time it takes time two to the n  and so we wondered maybe if you consider  shorter games maybe then  the backlog becomes smaller again  and so that led us to consider games  that were quasi-polynomial length so  games that have length 2 to the poly log  n  and kind of remarkably again it turns  out  the backlog is still huge  so what we were able to prove is for any  deterministic emptying algorithm  and for any constant parameter epsilon  greater than zero  there's a filling strategy strategy that  achieves backlog  n to the one minus epsilon within  quasi-polynomial time  and there's an interesting gap here  because of course the upper bound is big  o of n  and now our lower bound is n to the one  minus epsilon  and i think this is an interesting open  question whether or not  that epsilon is necessary i don't know  the answer  the next thing we started to wonder  about was well this emptier  this the second theorem assumes that the  empty is a deterministic algorithm  but what if the empty is a randomized  algorithm  why would that help well if the is a  randomized algorithm  then you assume that the filler is  what's known as an oblivious adversary  that means the filler's blind they can't  see what the emptier is doing  they can't see what the state of the  cups are  and that means that the empty can in  turn use their randomization to try to  make it so the filler doesn't know  what's going on  in order to try to hamper the filler's  efforts and keep the backlog small  what we considered in our paper were  randomized emptying algorithms that are  that satisfy a certain property known as  being greedy-like  so an emptying algorithm is greedy-like  what that means is  if it's trying to choose between two  cups to empty out of  and one of the cups contains  significantly more water than the other  then a greedy-like algorithm will always  prefer the cup that contains  significantly more water  but if the algorithm is choosing between  cups that have a similar amount of water  to each other  then the algorithm is allowed to use its  randomness and pick between those cups  however it wants  that's what it means to be a randomized  greedy-like algorithm  and what's interesting is again the  you know it doesn't really change the  outcome the randomization  doesn't help with the situation we're  still in a situation  where a filler can actually force a  backlog of n to the one minus epsilon  that's what we proved  and of course there's an interesting  open question here too which is  what happens if you remove this  greedy-like assumption what happens if  you consider  just an arbitrary randomized empty  algorithm  is there still a filling strategy that  forces a backlog of n to the one minus  epsilon  and again i don't know what the answer  to that should be  so this was where our results brought us  is that even if your empty is allowed to  be randomized  as long as they satisfy a little bit of  structure they're greedy-like  then still the filler can force a large  backlog  and when you put these results together  you start to get a kind of interesting  story  which is that these two games the  variable processor cup game  and the p processor cup game really are  fundamentally different  the backlog for the variable processor  cup game really is fundamentally larger  than for its p processor counterpart  and i think this raises an interesting  direction for future work  which is to try to understand whether  the separation between those two games  whether that persists in the presence of  beyond worst case analysis  so what if you consider resource  augmentation  what if you consider you know for  example smooth  analysis or restricting the rate at  which the filler can change  uh p over time you know  when you when you consider these forms  of beyond worst case analysis  is it still the case that these two  games are so different from each other  i think that's a fascinating question  okay so what i want to do in the rest of  the talk is i want to give you a sense  for  why the filler can cause such a large  backlog in the variable processor cup  game  how does develop how does the filler  achieve a backlog  of omega n  to be more precise our goal is going to  be to achieve a backlog of n minus 1  over 2  on incomes that's our goal  and the way we're going to do that is  with a recursive construction  and so we're going to have an inductive  hypothesis which is that we already know  how to do our goal  on n minus 1 cups what that means is  that on n minus  1 cups we know how to achieve a backlog  of n minus 2  over 2. then we're going to use this  inductive hypothesis recursively in  order to achieve our goal  on n cups now in order to be able to  visualize this inductive hypothesis the  way we're going to think of this is that  you start with n  minus 1 empty cups you apply this  golden box which represents 