f every recursive step it  has to figure out  what's the cup that actually has the  large amount of water that i was aiming  for  and so when you make the filler be an  oblivious adversary that really changes  the construction and kind of  one of the key technical challenges in  the paper was figuring out how  to come up with a construction for the  filler that worked against  randomized greedy-like emptying  algorithms  so those were some of the challenges  that we overcame in the paper  but the the core idea is still  basically this this idea that with a  recursive construction in the variable  processor cup game  the backlog becomes much larger than it  ever would have been  in the p processor cup game for any  fixed value of p  i want to conclude the talk by coming  back to this slide that we saw earlier  the slide with some directions for  future work and  now that we've actually seen a  construction for how the filler can  force such a large backlog in the  variable processor cup game  i think we can also now better  appreciate why these directions of work  seems so promising  and that's because this construction  seems kind of fragile  it's this recursive construction where  the number of processors p  varies in this kind of fractal-like way  and it seems like  it seems like that construction it it's  hard to tell whether that kind of  construction would be robust  to beyond worst case analysis  so what would happen for example with  resource augmentation what that means is  that now  the empty is actually allowed to remove  a little more water and aggregate than  the filler put in  in at each step or what happens for  example if we place  restrictions on the filler so for  example we restrict the rate at which p  varies i think these questions are kind  of  really fundamental questions if we want  to understand the difference between the  variable processor cup game  and its p processor counterpart thank  you very much  you
 

